  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On May 10-11, Trump publicly dismissed Iran's counter-proposal as "garbage" and described the ceasefire as on "massive life support" (Bloomberg, CNN, Al Jazeera, Axios). Separately, Pezeshkian said on Press TV that Iran would "never bow its head before the enemy" and that "dialogue does not mean surrender or retreat" — the first time the president addressed the negotiating framework in those terms since Iran filed its 14-point counter-proposal on May 2. Foreign Ministry spokesperson Baghaei added that the US maintained "unreasonable demands" and defended Iran's plan as "reasonable and generous" (Press TV, cited by CNBC and NPR). Both named Iranian officials met the resolution criteria for F1 — two independent outlets (Press TV and CNBC), two named officials (Pezeshkian and Baghaei), direct on-record engagement with the framework's terms. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The gap between the two public positions as of May 12 is structural: the US framework subordinates Hormuz reopening to nuclear-program commitments, while Iran's counter-proposal inverts the sequence — end the war and lift sanctions first, defer nuclear to a later stage. Baghaei stated explicitly that Iran's plan contained "no details regarding nuclear issues" (Press TV, May 11). Trump confirmed to reporters on May 11 that he would not discuss Iran plans before his China trip (NewsNation), making May 14-15 the next plausible diplomatic window per Axios. The six-day gap between the Trump-Xi summit and the May 18 memo deadline is the operative constraint on <span class="cite">F2</span>. → supports <span class="cite">F2</span> without move</p>

      <p>In Lebanon, the IDF's Golani Brigade conducted a week-long ground incursion beyond the Litani River through May 12, with the IDF reporting "operational control" over the Zawtar al-Sharqiyah area and 100+ airstrikes in south Lebanon (Times of Israel, May 12). UNIFIL confirmed this was the most intense attack tempo since the April 16 truce began. Al Jazeera (May 11) described the ceasefire as barely functional. No second Beirut governorate strike was confirmed between May 7-12. Separately, Smotrich resumed coalition voting after a reservist compensation compromise (Times of Israel) — the primary coalition-pressure driver for Beirut strikes is now partially neutralised. US-Israel-Lebanon talks are scheduled for May 14-15 in Washington (State Dept), creating a short diplomatic inhibition window. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Saudi Arabia and Kuwait formally lifted restrictions on US military access to their bases and airspace on May 7-8, following a second MBS-Trump phone call (Wall Street Journal, Iran International, Times of Israel). The restoration clears the path for Project Freedom restart and resolves F5 ahead of its May 25 horizon. Separately, Hormuz transit traffic remained at approximately 5-6 vessels/day as of May 3-5, and Lloyd's war-risk premiums held at 1.5-3% of hull value as of May 6 (Albany Antree War-Risk Insurance Update) — the binding insurance threshold for F6 is unmet. Brent crude settled at $104.97 on May 12 (Hart Energy), up from the $100 level on May 7, with the market re-pricing toward extended Hormuz closure following Trump's public rejection of Iran's proposal. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>, <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>Araghchi visited Beijing May 5-6, holding talks with Wang Yi who pressed Iran to pursue a diplomatic path and called for "an immediate end to hostilities" (CNBC, Al Jazeera). The visit pre-positioned Iran ahead of Trump's May 14-15 China trip. Intelligence reporting from CNN (May 8) described Mojtaba Khamenei as injured but "still shaping strategy" — Pezeshkian's May 7 claim of a two-and-a-half-hour meeting with Mojtaba was refuted by Iran International, which reported no such meeting took place. Mojtaba has made no verified public appearance since Day 61. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:30%;">Escalation 30%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:42%;">Protracted 42%</div>
        <div class="prob-deescalation prob-bar" style="width:28%;">De-esc 28%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (30%):</strong> Driven by F2 collapsing (0.15 YES = 0.85 NO-or-AMBIGUOUS by May 18) combined with F4 (38% second Beirut strike) and the risk that Trump's military-action deliberations described by Axios become operational before the Xi summit can intervene.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (42%):</strong> The most probable path: talks formally deadlock past May 18 without a signed memo and without Iran formally rejecting (F3 only 28%), freezing the conflict at current levels with periodic south Lebanon escalation and continued Hormuz disruption.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (28%):</strong> Requires the Trump-Xi summit to produce a bridging formula that persuades Tehran to revise its counter-proposal (F new-N1 at 30%), followed by a memo framework that accommodates the Hormuz-first sequencing Iran demands; Brent at $105 and rising creates US economic pressure in this direction.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Press TV's coverage of the May 10-11 exchange ran under headlines defending Iran's proposal as "reasonable and generous" — the same language Baghaei used in his on-camera statement. State media did not use the word "rejection" about the US framework; the framing was consistently that the US is the intransigent party demanding "unreasonable" concessions. This framing performs two functions for domestic audiences: it maintains the narrative that Iran is the diplomatically cooperative party while ruling out further concessions without explicit US movement. Pezeshkian's "never bow" formulation was carried on all major state-media channels and presented as a sovereignty statement, not a war posture — the distinction matters because it leaves a return to talks technically available. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
      <p>Mojtaba Khamenei's absence from all state-media coverage remains total. No audio, video, or attributed quote has emerged since Day 61. Iran International's refutation of Pezeshkian's meeting claim (May 7-8) went unreported on state media. CNN's May 8 intelligence assessment — "injured but still shaping strategy" — was also absent from state channels. The absence of any state-media correction or rebuttal of Pezeshkian's meeting claim is the only editorial response visible. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Kayhan had not published rejection-grade language about the US framework as of May 12 searches. The hardliner paper's silence on formal rejection, while Baghaei defends the plan publicly, is consistent with a regime posture of tactical patience rather than strategic escalation. The combination of Baghaei's "reasonable" framing and Kayhan's non-rejection is the narrow channel through which a revised counter-proposal could emerge post-Xi summit. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

